<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Transfer — Stress (distributed)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Stress test: system-wide throughput on POST /api/v1/transactions/transfer.

Each iteration debits a source account by $0.10 and credits a destination
account. Pairs come from tests/stress/data/transfers.csv (1000 rows). The
seed script pairs source[i] -&gt; dest[(i + SHIFT) % N] using SHIFT coprime
with N, near N/2 (499 for N=1000). This guarantees:

  - Every account is source of exactly ONE row and destination of exactly
    ONE (different) row — no SELF_TRANSFER_NOT_ALLOWED rejections.
  - Adjacent CSV rows share no accounts — neighbouring VUs in a
    round-robin reader don't pile up on the same pair, so contention is
    spread across the account graph rather than concentrated.

This stress test isolates the system: Node event loop, Prisma connection
pool, Postgres lock manager, network — whichever saturates first becomes
the breaking point. Transfer is HEAVY compared to the read endpoint: it
acquires FOR UPDATE on TWO accounts inside a $transaction, writes TWO
transaction rows (TRANSFER_OUT + TRANSFER_IN), and runs the fraud
detector. Expect the breaking point to arrive substantially earlier than
the 650 VUs the read endpoint sustained.

Shape (Stepping Thread Group) — sustained-capacity scan:
  - Start at 2 VUs (initial burst)
  - Add 2 VUs every 30s, ramp-up 1s per step
  - Peak at 50 VUs (worst-case ramp ~12:30)
  - Hold 120s at peak

This is the THIRD stress shape attempt for transfer, and the rationale
for the slow step is important:

  Run 1 (+50/10s, cap 2000): clean to 300 VUs, 100% errors at 450 VUs.
  Run 2 (+10/10s, cap 200):  clean to 60 VUs, 100% errors at 110 VUs.
  Run 3 (this shape):        +2 VUs every 30s, cap 50, hold 120s.

The wildly different boundaries from run 1 and run 2 — measured on
identical clean state (run-stress.mjs reseeds + restarts every
invocation) — proved that the TRANSIENT boundary (what +50/10s
measures) is not the same as the SUSTAINED boundary (what longer
holds measure). The transfer write path holds row locks inside a
Prisma $transaction; under realistic-pacing concurrency the lock
queue grows linearly over time, and once the queue grows past the
default 5s transaction timeout, every request times out and the
system collapses irreversibly.

Run 2's "60 VUs clean" was a 10s observation — not long enough for
the queue to compound. The transfer.load.jmx run that was sized to
B=60 produced ~95% errors at 42 VUs (70% of 60) within the first
30s of sustained load, confirming sustained capacity is far below
the transient measurement.

This stress run gives each VU level 30s of steady state to either
stabilize or saturate. The first step where errors persist after
the 30s settle window is the SUSTAINED boundary — the right number
for load/spike planning.

Step layout:
  - Each step adds 2 VUs (vs +10 in run 2) for finer resolution at
    the lower end where the boundary now lives
  - 30s per step (vs 10s) so each level has time to expose lock-
    queue accumulation, fraud-table growth, and connection-pool
    state, rather than just thread-start jitter
  - Cap 50 (vs 200): based on run 2's load-test outcome, sustained
    capacity is well under 50; the cap is generous headroom

Expected outcome: errors first appear somewhere in the low-double-
digit VU range. Whatever VU level survives 30s with &lt;1% errors AND
the next step breaks is the sustained boundary.

Pass criteria: no 5xx, no timeouts, all responses are 2xx or 422.
Breaking point: first step where p95 &gt; 5000ms OR error rate &gt; 1%.

p95 is a report metric (Aggregate Report / HTML dashboard), not a
per-sample assertion. The only per-sample gate is the response-code
assertion — any HTTP failure or timeout counts toward the 1% error
budget.

Amount: $0.10 per transfer — deliberately below every business limit:
  - Single transfer max: $50,000 ✓
  - Daily transfer limit (CHECKING): $10,000  At ~600 req/s aggregate
    across 1000 source accounts, each account sees ~0.6 transfers/s ×
    60s = ~36 × $0.10 = $3.60 per minute, orders of magnitude under
    the rolling-24h cap.
  - Min: $0.01 ✓
  - Fraud LARGE_AMOUNT_SINGLE ($&gt;3000): not triggered ✓
  - Fraud OFFHOURS_LARGE ($&gt;1000): not triggered ✓
  - Fraud RAPID_BALANCE_DRAIN (&gt;90% of balance): not triggered
    ($0.10 / $1M = 1e-7) ✓
  - Fraud NEW_ACCOUNT_LARGE ($&gt;500): not triggered ✓
  - Fraud ROUND_AMOUNT (no cents): not triggered (0.10 has cents) ✓

Fraud signals that WILL fire under sustained per-account velocity:
  - VELOCITY_3_IN_5MIN (40 pts) after the 3rd debit per source in 5min
  - VELOCITY_10_IN_1HR (30 pts) after the 10th debit per source in 1h
  Total max: 70 pts → REVIEW_FLAGGED (allowed, status 200/201, just
  flagged). Score never reaches the FRAUD_BLOCKED threshold of 100 at
  these amounts. So fraud signals appear in the DB but do not cause
  transaction failures.

⚠ Weekend caveat (banking-rules §4): transfers initiated on Saturday or
Sunday UTC are routed to PENDING_WEEKEND (status 201, queued for Monday
09:00 UTC) and do NOT execute the full lock+update+insert path. If this
test runs on Sat/Sun UTC, the response codes will be 201 (passing the
assertion) but the throughput numbers measure the queuing path, not the
real transfer path. Run on a weekday for meaningful numbers.

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms — total delay ≈ 3000ms + |N(0, 1000)| per
iteration). Deliberate choice: the goal is to measure capacity under
REALISTIC user pacing, not synthetic peak throughput. A no-think-time
stress test models a DDoS-style attacker; with the timer this measures
how many concurrent NORMAL users the system can sustain. Consistent
with the read / withdraw / deposit stress plans.

Load-generator memory: 50 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈ 50 MB at
peak — trivial, well within the default JMeter heap (1 GB).

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/stress/transfer.stress.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/transfer.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/transfer-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:stress:transfer

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts          (generates transfers.csv)
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(host,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(dataDir,tests/stress/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <ConfigTestElement guiclass="HttpDefaultsGui" testclass="ConfigTestElement" testname="HTTP Request Defaults">
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">5000</intProp>
        <intProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">15000</intProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${BASE_URL}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${PORT}</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables">
          <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
        </elementProp>
        <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.implementation"></stringProp>
      </ConfigTestElement>
      <hashTree/>
      <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager">
        <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="" elementType="Header">
            <stringProp name="Header.name">Authorization</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Header.value">Bearer ${sourceToken}</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </HeaderManager>
      <hashTree/>
      <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="transfers.csv">
        <stringProp name="filename">${DATA_DIR}/transfers.csv</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="variableNames">sourceAccountId,destinationAccountId,sourceUserId,sourceToken</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="quotedData">false</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
        <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
        <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
      </CSVDataSet>
      <hashTree/>
      <kg.apc.jmeter.threads.SteppingThreadGroup guiclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.SteppingThreadGroupGui" testclass="kg.apc.jmeter.threads.SteppingThreadGroup" testname="Stepping Thread Group — 2 burst, +2 every 30s, peak 50, hold 120s">
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.num_threads">50</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Threads initial delay">0</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Start users count">2</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Start users count burst">2</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Start users period">30</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Stop users count">5</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="Stop users period">2</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="flighttime">120</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="rampUp">1</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="LoopController" guiclass="LoopControlPanel" testclass="LoopController" testname="Loop Controller">
          <intProp name="LoopController.loops">-1</intProp>
          <boolProp name="LoopController.continue_forever">false</boolProp>
        </elementProp>
      </kg.apc.jmeter.threads.SteppingThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="POST /api/v1/transactions/transfer">
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/api/v1/transactions/transfer</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">false</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">{&quot;sourceAccountId&quot;:&quot;${sourceAccountId}&quot;,&quot;destinationAccountId&quot;:&quot;${destinationAccountId}&quot;,&quot;amount&quot;:&quot;0.10&quot;}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <GaussianRandomTimer guiclass="GaussianRandomTimerGui" testclass="GaussianRandomTimer" testname="Think Time — Gaussian (mean 3s, σ 1s)">
            <stringProp name="ConstantTimer.delay">3000</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="RandomTimer.range">1000.0</stringProp>
          </GaussianRandomTimer>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="Status is 2xx or 422" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="1729207179">^(2\d\d|422)$</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Server error or unexpected status — transfer flow broke under load.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">1</intProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
        <ResultCollector guiclass="StatVisualizer" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Aggregate Report" enabled="true">
          <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
          <objProp>
            <name>saveConfig</name>
            <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
